WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:06.840
From an exceedingly evil Ursula to horrifying&nbsp;
references from the original tragic fairy tale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:11.340
there are a stack of gruesome changes&nbsp;
and sinister secrets hidden in Disney's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.500
live-action remake of The Little Mermaid.
Spoilers ahead, of course, so take care.
&nbsp;

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.760
Let's kick things off with Ursula
– one of Disney's most iconic villains
&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.320
– and the new movie really amps up&nbsp;
what a despicable baddie she truly is.
&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.280
So when we first meet her this time,&nbsp;
she's complaining about wasting away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:31.500
in exile while spearing some shrimp with her&nbsp;
fingernail and chomping it down as a snack.
&nbsp;

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.820
This addition is also a grisly little&nbsp;
bit of foreshadowing for her own death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.480
by the end when she gets stuck with&nbsp;
the pointy end of a ship's mast.
&nbsp;

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.680
And for the remake, Ursula's lair&nbsp;
has acquired some even scarier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.300
creatures compared to the animated version.
Gone are the polyps which contained the souls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:54.000
of merfolk that came back to life after Ursula&nbsp;
died, and these have been replaced by nightmarish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.400
single-eyed Lovecraftian monsters that remind&nbsp;
me of the monster in the Doctor Strange sequel.
&nbsp;

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.900
These new creatures grab Ariel by&nbsp;
the neck at one point in a deep-cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.160
reference to the original fairy tale where&nbsp;
one of the polyps outside the sea witch's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.900
house strangled a little mermaid to death.
As for why the filmmakers cut the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.640
movie's polyps, Director Rob Marshall thought&nbsp;
that seeing them turn back into people was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.080
something that could only work in animation,&nbsp;
and that Triton turning into a polyp would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.880
be too comical for a live-action movie.
In the new movie, the floor in Ursula's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:32.640
cave is littered with skulls and even the entire&nbsp;
skeleton of a merperson, proving that the fate of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.800
those who've crossed Ursula is now even worse than&nbsp;
those poor unfortunate souls in the animation.
&nbsp;

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:43.680
This new-look lair is also a nod to the original&nbsp;
fairy tale where the sea witch's house was located&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.960
in the middle of a forest of serpent-like&nbsp;
half-animal, half-plant polyps with long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.360
arms that trapped the skeletons of dead humans and&nbsp;
animals which had sunk to the bottom of the sea.
&nbsp;

00:01:54.360 --> 00:02:00.060
In fact, it's implied later on when Flotsam and&nbsp;
Jetsam electrocute King Triton, draining him of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:05.520
life that that's how Ursula kills her victims.
By the way, the skeleton of the whale-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.980
creature that forms Ursula's new lair is based&nbsp;
on unused concept art from the animated movie.
&nbsp;

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:16.560
Live-action Ursula's transformation into a&nbsp;
giant kaiju-like monster was very impressive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.280
although her death in the remake is a&nbsp;
little less grisly than the animation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.380
with the camera cutting away when the ship's&nbsp;
spike pierces the giant villain, and also her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:30.960
not getting electrocuted by lightning this time.
In the new movie though, Ursula does become even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.420
more monstrous, with gruesome gills added&nbsp;
to her cheeks and huge deadened eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.500
possibly a hat-tip to her evil red eyes&nbsp;
in this scene in the animated movie.
&nbsp;

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.540
When Disney first adapted Hans Christian&nbsp;
Andersen's fairy tale for their animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.380
classic, they softened many of the&nbsp;
darker edges of the original story;
&nbsp;

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.920
however, many of these are back&nbsp;
in various forms in the new film.
&nbsp;

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:59.160
So, during Ariel's transformation, listen very&nbsp;
carefully and you'll hear tearing sounds as her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:03.840
tail rips and splits into two, a shout-out&nbsp;
to Andersen's original story where the sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.520
witch tells the little mermaid that when the&nbsp;
potion transforms her fin into human legs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.420
she will feel great pain as if a&nbsp;
sword were passing through her body. 
&nbsp;

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:17.160
And later on when Scuttle asks Sebastian&nbsp;
whether Ariel has killed the prince yet,
&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.900
it's a sneaky, sinister reference to how&nbsp;
in the fairy tale when the little mermaid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:26.760
failed to gain the prince's true love, her&nbsp;
mermaid sisters struck a deal with the sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.240
witch that if she killed the prince with&nbsp;
a dagger and put his blood on to her feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.880
she would be able to turn back into a mermaid. 
In Andersen's story, if the prince ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.620
marrying another woman, the little mermaid's fate&nbsp;
would be to die the next morning and become the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:46.260
foam that floats on the crest of the ocean waves.
So when the movie's opening scene is of giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.340
foaming waves, it's no coincidence.
At the start, there's also a quote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:53.940
from Andersen's fairy tale:
"but a mermaid has no tears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:58.260
and therefore she suffers so much more",
a moment which pays off at the very end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.820
when in her human form Ariel sheds a&nbsp;
tear as she says goodbye to her father.
&nbsp;

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:08.220
Likewise the fairy tale also ends with the&nbsp;
little mermaid's tears as she refuses to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:14.580
kill the prince and so gains an eternal soul.
A small, but significant and bloody change in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:20.220
live-action is how Ariel agrees to her deal with&nbsp;
Ursula by signing it in blood after plucking a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:25.020
scale from her own tail, rather than signing&nbsp;
her name as she did in the animated movie. 
&nbsp;

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.820
This sidesteps a commonly cited plot&nbsp;
hole from the animation that Ariel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:34.320
could have easily explained to Eric why she&nbsp;
was mute by writing down how she'd lost her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:40.020
voice and that she was the one who saved him.
In tune with many of Disney's live-action remakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.720
some of the wackier, more&nbsp;
slapstick elements from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.880
animation have been removed from this version.
An unfortunate casualty is French chef Louis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:53.280
notorious for his obsessive and ridiculously&nbsp;
OTT efforts to catch and cook the little crab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.720
Sebastian, who's been chopped out of the new&nbsp;
film, meaning of course that the song "Les&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.361
Poissons" is also missing.
How I love les poissons.
&nbsp;

00:04:59.361 --> 00:05:06.420
Love to chop and to serve little fish.
There is, however, a nod to that sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:10.320
in both the castle's fishy tiles&nbsp;
and when Sebastian says he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.960
have been fricasseed trying to reach Ariel.
In the animated movie, one of the thingamabobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.740
that Ariel found [in the sunken ship]&nbsp;
was a tobacco pipe that was amusingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:24.960
named and described by Scuttle 
A banded, bulbous snarfblatt.
&nbsp;

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:30.720
However, since 2007 Disney has banned the&nbsp;
depiction of any smoking products in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.840
of it movies rated PG-13 or less, so all of the&nbsp;
amusing animated scenes featuring the snarfblatt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:42.240
have been completely banished from the remake.
The live-action movie reveals that Ursula is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:47.760
actually Triton's sister, making her dastardly&nbsp;
scheme against Ariel – her own niece – feel even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:51.120
worse and more malevolent.
The film also explains that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.260
Ariel's mother was killed by humans;
however, what was left out but explained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.260 --> 00:06:00.180
in the movie's novelisation is that Ursula's&nbsp;
actions were also responsible for that death.
&nbsp;

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:05.700
Apparently, 15 years earlier, the sea witch&nbsp;
shapeshifted into the form of Triton's wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:09.360
the Sea Queen, and then attacked&nbsp;
a human ship, but was caught.
&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.400
And when Ariel's mother decided to help&nbsp;
Ursula escape, she ended up dying.
&nbsp;

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:17.700
This was the event that led Triton&nbsp;
to banish his wicked sister.
&nbsp;

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:22.800
And it also appears that Eric's birth family&nbsp;
was killed during Ursula's attack and she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:27.360
therefore the reason why he was shipwrecked&nbsp;
and orphaned, and ended up being adopted.
&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.760
Similar to the Lion King, the live-action&nbsp;
Little Mermaid strives for a photorealistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:38.100
world in its depiction of life under the sea.
Although this is a technically impressive feat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:42.480
it does mean a good deal of the expressive&nbsp;
emotions conveyed by the animated animals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:46.920
isn't translated into live action.
Of all the characters, Flounder suffered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:51.480
the most from this and the internet completely&nbsp;
freaked out when they saw him for the first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:56.580
as visually he simply doesn't have the fun&nbsp;
and vibrancy of his cartoon counterpart.
&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:00.120
By the way, I thought it was a little&nbsp;
shocking how Scuttle quite casually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:04.020
swooped into the water in front of Ariel&nbsp;
and Flounder to snack on some fish.
&nbsp;

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:09.780
Obviously, gannets and other birds do eat fish&nbsp;
in real life, but it was a little weird how Ariel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:12.960
wasn't shocked at the horror of this,
You OK Flounder?
&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.285
Sure, you gotta show 'em who's in charge.
Ahh!
&nbsp;

00:07:15.285 --> 00:07:17.640
[Ariel laughs]
It's just Scuttle!
&nbsp;

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:23.220
while being clearly perturbed later on at seeing&nbsp;
pictures of fish on hooks in the human world.
&nbsp;

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:29.040
Sebastian's new look is based on the Cuban Land&nbsp;
Crab and he has a much larger front right claw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.060
than his left claw, another change made to&nbsp;
make the crab as realistic as possible.
&nbsp;

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:37.260
Because he has larger eyes than his fish&nbsp;
friends though, he still has a little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:41.340
ability to emote, though it can't quite&nbsp;
compare to the animation in this regard.
&nbsp;

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:45.900
And while the live-action version of&nbsp;
"Under the Sea" is visually spectacular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:50.160
indeed it looks just gorgeous and colourful,&nbsp;
and it's very impressive, and reminded me of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:55.680
some of Busby Berkeley's choreography, it doesn't&nbsp;
quite hit the comedic notes of the original song&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.000
as it strives to be more photoreal.
There's definitely a lot of interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.000
changes in the remake, and I'd love&nbsp;
to hear what you thought of them.
&nbsp;

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:07.140
I especially enjoyed Halle Bailey&nbsp;
and Melissa McCarthy's performances.
&nbsp;

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:10.080
And if you enjoyed this&nbsp;
video, do leave a thumbs-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:14.700
and subscribe to get more videos like this.
Thanks for enjoying The Little Mermaid with me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:18.900
and hope you have a marvellous movie-loving week!
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

